  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 159 PM EDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nAfternoon water vapor and RAP analysis continue to feature deep \nmidlevel troughing over the Four Corners, and ridging expanding out \nover the Canadian Prairie. An embedded shortwave  Closer to home, \nspotty rain showers continue across the eastern half of the UP with \na cold front directed from near Wausau to the Soo. However, some \nlight radar returns continue to drift over the central and western \nUP, though dewpoint depressions on the order of 10-15 degrees so far \nis limiting the potential for much of anything to reach the ground. \nUnder cloudy skies, temperatures are coming in a couple degrees \ncooler than expected across the western UP; temperatures there may \nonly peak in the lower to mid 50s this afternoon. To the east, \ntemperatures are quickly falling back into the 50s and even upper \n40s behind the front after having climbed as high as the upper 50s \nand even lower 60s earlier today.\n\nAs we head into the evening hours and the front drops to our south, \nrain showers largely wrap up, though some spotty activity is not out \nof the question closer to the WI border through tonight. \nTemperatures fall back into the mid to upper 30s under cloudy skies, \nthough some of the typically cooler spots in the north-central UP \nmay fall a couple degrees further into the lower 30s.  \n\nRidging begins to recover Monday as the Canadian high becomes \ncentered over Ontario. At the same time, however, the Four Corners \ntrough lifts northward into the Northern Plains by Monday evening, \nsupporting a weak low to the west which may graze the western UP \nwith some rain showers Monday night through Tuesday afternoon. This \nshortwave lifts north into Ontario Tuesday night, but low-end PoPs \ncontinue throughout the day Wednesday as another surface low moves \nthrough Manitoba and into Ontario. This drags a cold front through \nthe UP Wednesday night, kicking off better chances for showers. \nGuidance quickly begins to struggle after Thursday as flow turns \nmore zonal and leaves us sensitive to any shortwave action, but a \nmore active period is anticipated to continue into early November \ncaptured by the CPC's 6-10 precipitation outlook of slightly above \nnormal precip. At this time, ensembles don't suggest any widespread \nmeaningful rain across the area with 24hr prob of QPF >0.1\" only \nreaching 60% on any given day. Additionally, above normal \ntemperatures dominate through the rest of the month into early \nNovember with seemingly no end in sight. All global ensembles \nhighlight strong positive 850mb temp anomalies even into their \nextended 200+ hour range.",
